<header><p class="enc-kicker">Institution</p><h1 class="enc-title">Tretyakov Gallery</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">1856</span> · Also known as State Tretyakov Gallery</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Tretyakov Gallery is an art museum in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/moscow">Moscow</a> holding the world's foremost collection of Russian art. It grew from the collection of the merchant Pavel Tretyakov, who began buying works in 1856 and gave the gallery to the city of Moscow in 1892. Its holdings include medieval icons such as <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/our-lady-of-vladimir">Our Lady of Vladimir</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/andrei-rublev">Andrei Rublev</a>'s Trinity.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/tretyakov-gallery.jpg" alt="Tretyakov Gallery" width="320" height="165" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: A.Savin · CC BY-SA 3.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Moscow_05-2012_TretyakovGallery.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
